6.1 WATER QUALITY INDEX (WQI)

Implement a transparent and configurable Water Quality Index calculation engine.

Use the Weighted Arithmetic Water Quality Index (WAWQI) as the initial methodology.

For the conventional weighted arithmetic method:

Quality rating:

Qi = [(Vi - V0) / (Si - V0)] × 100

Unit weight:

Wi = K / Si

Where:

K = 1 / Σ(1 / Si)

Final WQI:

WQI = Σ(Wi × Qi) / ΣWi

Where:

Vi = measured value of a water-quality parameter.

V0 = ideal value for that parameter.

Si = selected permissible or standard value.

Wi = unit weight.

Qi = quality rating.

Important scientific rules

Select the intended water use before calculating WQI:

Drinking water.

Bathing.

Surface water.

Irrigation.

Aquatic ecosystem monitoring.

Use the appropriate official standard for that water use and geographical jurisdiction.

For India, support CPCB Designated Best Use criteria for relevant surface-water classifications.

Use BIS/WHO-based standards where appropriate for drinking-water assessment. Do not treat surface-water criteria as drinking-water standards.

Handle parameters according to their direction:

pH is a two-sided parameter and should be assessed against the permitted range.

DO is generally a higher-is-better parameter, so use an appropriate direction-aware calculation.

Parameters such as BOD, COD, turbidity and nitrate generally increase concern as their values rise.

Do not apply one universal ideal DO value to all water samples. Account for relevant conditions such as temperature and salinity where the chosen methodology requires them.

Show the contribution of every included parameter.

Never treat missing values as zero.

If essential parameters are missing, display the WQI as incomplete or provisional, with the coverage percentage.

Display microbiological failures separately. A favourable aggregate WQI must never hide a potentially dangerous coliform result.

Save the selected standard, standard version, calculation method and calculation date with every WQI result.

