7.1 WASTE MANAGEMENT INDEX (WMI)

Implement the following proposed EcoIntelligence Waste Management Index.

This is a project-defined composite index, not an official universal international standard.

The WMI must range from 0 to 100.

Higher WMI = Better waste management performance.

Use these six components:

Collection Efficiency – 20% weight.

Segregation Efficiency – 15% weight.

Recycling and Recovery Rate – 25% weight.

Treatment Efficiency – 15% weight.

Safe Disposal Rate – 15% weight.

Unmanaged Waste Control – 10% weight.

Formulas:

Collection Efficiency:

C = (Waste Collected / Total Waste Generated) × 100

Segregation Efficiency:

S = (Correctly Segregated Waste / Waste Collected) × 100

Recycling and Recovery Rate:

R = (Waste Recycled or Recovered / Total Waste Generated) × 100

Treatment Efficiency:

T = (Non-recycling Waste Treated / Total Waste Generated) × 100

Safe Disposal Rate:

D = (Residual Waste Safely Disposed / Total Waste Generated) × 100

Unmanaged Waste Control:

U = 100 - (Unmanaged Waste / Total Waste Generated × 100)

Final WMI:

WMI = (0.20 × C) + (0.15 × S) + (0.25 × R) + (0.15 × T) + (0.15 × D) + (0.10 × U)

WMI Classification

Use these initial project-defined bands:

80–100: Excellent.

60–79.99: Good.

40–59.99: Moderate.

20–39.99: Poor.

0–19.99: Critical.

Clearly identify these as EcoIntelligence's proposed classification bands, not official regulatory ratings.

WMI Validation Rules

Total generated waste must be greater than zero.

Collected waste must not exceed generated waste unless documented imports or reporting differences explain it.

Segregated waste must not exceed collected waste.

All waste quantities must use consistent units and reporting periods.

Recycling, treatment, safe disposal and unmanaged waste must be treated as mutually exclusive final destinations when calculating the waste-flow balance.

Do not double-count waste in multiple destination categories.

Check that generated waste is approximately equal to the sum of accounted waste destinations and other documented flows.

Flag unexplained differences in the mass balance.

Never treat missing values as zero.

If data is incomplete, show an incomplete or provisional WMI with its component coverage.

Store the methodology version and component calculations.

